Q: Are compilers allowed to merge nested virtual calls inside a &quot;final&quot; class? A: <p>Try an intermediate CRTP instead of an intermediate polymorphic class.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;class T, class D&gt;
struct DerivedStatic : Base {
  void Call(void* p) final {
    self()-&gt;CallTyped( *static_cast&lt;int*&gt;(p) );
  }
  D* self() { return static_cast&lt;D*&gt;(this); }
};
</code></pre>
<p>While devirtualization is legal, proving nobody else knows about your virtual function is difficult, and if they know about it the function, and table entry has to exist.  Even the function name!  And virtual member function pointers add yet another complication.</p>
<p>So it can be done, I am unaware of compilers that do it, nor a way to express that it is safe to do.</p>
<p>But the CRTP trick above does what you want.  No 2nd virtual call occurs or can occur, and bog-standard inlining removes the non-virtual call and places the full implementation within <code>Call</code>.</p>
<pre><code>struct Bottom:
  DerivedStatic&lt;int, Bottom&gt;
{
  void CallTyped(int*){}
};
</code></pre>

Q: Using Angular&#39;s @switch() that shares a template for several @case? A: <p>Looks like <a href="https://angular.dev/api/core/@switch" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Angular 21</a> (I think that once Angular 22 will pop out, <a href="https://v21.angular.dev/api/core/@switch" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this one</a> will be the correct URL) supports the list of cases with the same behavior:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>@switch (condition) {
  @case (caseA) {
    Case A.
  }
  @case (caseB)
  @case (caseC) {
    Case B or C.
  }
  @default {
    Default case.
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Storage duration of compound literals in C A: <blockquote>
<p>Storage duration of compound literals in C</p>
</blockquote>
<p>With the below code, <em>compound literal</em> <code>(int []){a, b}</code> exists from its creation to the closing <code>}</code>.  In this case that is also where the function ends.  The <em>address</em> to that compound literal (saved in <code>foo-&gt;i</code>) ceases to be valid then too.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f(struct s *foo, const int a, const int b)
{
    foo-&gt;i = (int []){a, b};
} // &lt;-- Valid until here.
</code></pre>
<p>After that point, dereferencing that address is <em>undefined behavior</em> (UB), as  the calling code does later with <code>foo.i[0]</code>.</p>
<p>Technically, even <em>reading</em> that address is also <em>undefined behavior</em> (UB), yet I have never come across a  system that did not tolerate the read of an invalid object address.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Since the code above runs without error, does it mean that the compilers created the array (int []){34, 35} in the global scope, therefore it has static storage duration?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;code above runs without error&quot; is an incorrect conclusion.  OP's observed results are reliant on UB, which may differ in the future.</p>
<p><code>(int []){34, 35}</code> in the global scope --&gt; no.<br />
... therefore it has static storage --&gt; duration? --&gt; no.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Should I stop using clang lol (jk)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No.</p>
<hr />
<p>Alternatives include:</p>
<p><strong>Allocate</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// foo-&gt;i = (int []){a, b};

#define CL (int []){a, b}
foo-&gt;i = malloc(sizeof CL);
if (foo-&gt;i == NULL) {
  Handle_OOM_with TBD_code();
} else {
  memcpy(foo-&gt;i, &amp;CL, sizeof CL);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Make static</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>void f(struct s *foo, const int a, const int b)
{
    static int ar[2];
    ar[0] = a;
    ar[1] = b;
    foo-&gt;i = &amp;ar;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note solutions implications - the allocation likely needs a matching deallocation somewhere, and the static-variable approach is only usable if we don't need two of these structures with different content simultaneously.  <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79870263/storage-duration-of-compound-literals-in-c/79870383?noredirect=1#comment140947945_79870383">@Toby Speight</a>.</p>

Q: How to pre-allocate (reserve) space on disk for a file on a macOS APFS volume, without root A: <p>One solution is to use <code>fcntl(F_PREALLOCATE)</code>, but that <strong>requires root permissions</strong>, and it's not clear why, as it's not doing anything different from just writing the data:</p>
<pre><code>int main(int argc, const char * argv[]) {
    int fd = open (&quot;/tmp/allocated&quot;, O_WRONLY + O_CREAT + O_TRUNC, 0644);
    if (fd &lt; 0) {
        perror(&quot;open&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    int res;
    off_t size = 0x40000000LL * 10; // 10 GB
    res = ftruncate(fd, size);
    if (res &lt; 0) {
        perror(&quot;ftruncate&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    fstore_t info = {};
    info.fst_flags = F_ALLOCATEALL | F_ALLOCATEPERSIST;
    info.fst_posmode = F_PEOFPOSMODE;
    info.fst_offset = 0;
    info.fst_length = size;
    res = fcntl (fd, F_PREALLOCATE, &amp;info);
    if (res &lt; 0) {
        perror(&quot;F_PREALLOCATE&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    #if 0
        // just write to the file
        off_t bufsize = 0x40000000LL;
        void *buf = malloc(bufsize);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; ++i) {
            off_t written = write (fd, buf, bufsize);
            if (written != bufsize) {
                perror(&quot;write&quot;);
                return 1;
            }
        }
    #endif
    res = close(fd);
    if (res &lt; 0) {
        perror(&quot;close&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that the <code>fcntl</code> call works only if the file has been expanded, e.g. with <code>ftruncate</code>, first.</p>
<p>Furthermore, the above code doesn't appear to really allocate the space on the device, as the following code will return -1 in the <code>l2p_devoffset</code> field. To fix that, one needs to actually write to the file as far as I could find out.</p>
<pre><code>    // Get extent(s) for the file
    fd = open (&quot;/tmp/allocated&quot;, O_RDONLY, 0);
    if (fd &lt; 0) {
        perror(&quot;open #2&quot;);
        return 1;
    }
    struct log2phys l2p = { 0, 0, 0 };
    res = fcntl (fd, F_LOG2PHYS_EXT, &amp;l2p);
    if (res == -1) {
        perror(&quot;F_LOG2PHYS_EXT&quot;);
    } else {
        // Note l2p_devoffset gives -1 unless I actually write data to the file, i.e. F_PREALLOCATE is not sufficient.
        printf(&quot;Extent len %lld at device ofs %lld\n&quot;, l2p.l2p_contigbytes, l2p.l2p_devoffset);
    }
    close(fd);

</code></pre>
<p>Which, overall, means that there's no way around actually writing to the file, unless one has root permissions and doesn't need to know where the extent of the file is on the device.</p>

